preset-mystery-metal-list-ask = What metal is the block made of?
preset-mystery-metal-list-title = Which metal is it?
preset-mystery-metal-list-question = Here is a block of an unknown metal. We find its density ρ = m/V and look the metal up in a table of densities. The scales give the mass; the measuring cylinder the volume — by how far the water rises. Let us start: weigh the cylinder with water.
preset-mystery-metal-plan-label = Unknown metal
preset-floating-conditions-list-ask = When does a body sink, float inside a liquid, come up?
preset-floating-conditions-list-title = When does a body sink, and when does it float?
preset-floating-conditions-list-question = A stoppered test tube, sand and a measuring cylinder of water. We fill the tube with sand to the brim and put it into the water. Then we pour part of the sand out and put it in again, and then pour out more. Each time we measure its mass (the scales: how much the reading grows) and its volume (the cylinder: how far the water rises), watch what happens to it and compare its mean density with that of water. When does a body sink, float inside a liquid, come up?
preset-floating-conditions-plan-label = Test tube full of sand
preset-floating-conditions-plan-label-2 = Run 2: pour part of the sand out
preset-floating-conditions-plan-label-3 = Test tube with less sand
preset-floating-conditions-plan-label-4 = Run 3: pour out more sand
preset-floating-conditions-plan-label-5 = Test tube with a pinch of sand
preset-floating-conditions-list-choices = It sinks when its density is greater than the liquid’s; floats inside when equal; comes up when less
preset-floating-conditions-list-choices-2 = A heavy body sinks and a light one comes up, whatever the liquid
preset-floating-conditions-list-choices-3 = A small body sinks and a large one comes up
preset-hydrostatic-weighing-list-ask = What substance is the block made of?
preset-hydrostatic-weighing-list-title = Hydrostatic weighing: what is the block made of?
preset-hydrostatic-weighing-list-question = Here is a block of an unknown substance, and there is no measuring cylinder for its volume — only a dynamometer and a beaker of water. We weigh the block in the air, then in water. The buoyant force equals the weight of the water it pushes aside, so it gives the volume, and then the density. What is the block made of?
preset-hydrostatic-weighing-plan-label = Unknown block
preset-archimedes-salt-list-title = Where is the buoyant force larger?
preset-archimedes-salt-list-question = We hang an aluminium block (250 g) on a dynamometer — first in the air, then lowered into water, then into the same water with salt dissolved in it. Where will the buoyant force be larger?
preset-archimedes-salt-list-choices = In fresh water
preset-archimedes-salt-list-choices-2 = In salt water
preset-archimedes-salt-list-choices-3 = The same
preset-mass-fraction-list-title = Make a solution with 10 % salt by mass
preset-mass-fraction-list-question = Let us make 50 g of a table-salt solution in which salt is 10 % of the mass. The beaker already holds 45 g of water. We weigh it, add 5 g of salt, let it dissolve and weigh again. What mass fraction of salt does the solution have?
preset-mass-fraction-list-choices = 5 %
preset-mass-fraction-list-choices-2 = 10 %
preset-mass-fraction-list-choices-3 = ≈ 11 %
preset-mass-fraction-list-choices-4 = 50 %
preset-grow-crystals-list-title = Growing copper sulfate crystals
preset-salt-controls-label = Copper sulfate
preset-hot-controls-label = Heat to
preset-build-question = We put { $salt } g of copper sulfate into 100 mL of water — at 20 °C that much will not dissolve. We heat it to { $hot } °C until all of it dissolves, then put the beaker in a fridge and cool it to 20 °C. How much copper sulfate comes out as crystals?
preset-build-choices = None — it stays dissolved
preset-build-choices-2 = Part of it
preset-build-choices-3 = All of it — { $salt } g
preset-heat-of-solution-list-title = A heater or a cooler?
preset-heat-of-solution-list-question = Into a beaker of 100 mL of water at room temperature we dissolve 20 g of ammonium chloride, and into another such beaker 10 g of sodium hydroxide. We watch the thermometer. How will the temperature of the solutions change?
preset-heat-of-solution-plan-label = Stir and watch the thermometer
preset-heat-of-solution-plan-label-2 = Take a second beaker with 100 mL of water
preset-heat-of-solution-list-choices = Both will cool down
preset-heat-of-solution-list-choices-2 = The first cools, the second warms up
preset-heat-of-solution-list-choices-3 = Both will warm up
preset-heat-of-solution-list-choices-4 = It will not change
preset-supersaturated-list-ask = What happens to the solution as it cools — and when a crystal is dropped in?
preset-supersaturated-list-title = What will one crystal do?
preset-supersaturated-list-question = A test tube holds 20 g of sodium acetate and 4 mL of water: most of the crystals do not dissolve. We heat it over a spirit lamp to 70 °C until all of it dissolves and let it cool in the room. Then we drop a tiny crystal of sodium acetate into the solution. What will happen?
preset-supersaturated-plan-label = Cool in the room to 22 °C
preset-supersaturated-plan-label-2 = A seed crystal
preset-supersaturated-plan-label-3 = Watch the thermometer
preset-supersaturated-list-choices = Crystals come out while it cools
preset-supersaturated-list-choices-2 = It stays liquid, and the crystal makes it crystallise and warm up
preset-supersaturated-list-choices-3 = It stays liquid, and the crystal makes it crystallise and cool down
preset-supersaturated-list-choices-4 = It stays liquid even with the crystal
preset-ice-latent-heat-list-ask = Will the experiment give the table value of λ for ice?
preset-ice-latent-heat-list-title = How much heat does it take to melt ice?
preset-ice-latent-heat-list-question = We pour 150 g of warm water into a calorimeter and wait until it warms up together with the water. Then we drop in 20 g of ice (−1 °C) and wait until the ice melts and the temperature settles. From the heat balance we find the specific latent heat of fusion of ice λ. Will it come out as in the table?
preset-ice-latent-heat-plan-label = Ice
preset-ice-latent-heat-plan-label-2 = Wait for the ice to melt
preset-ice-latent-heat-list-choices = Yes, ≈ 334 kJ/kg
preset-ice-latent-heat-list-choices-2 = Less than in the table
preset-ice-latent-heat-list-choices-3 = More than in the table
preset-specific-heat-metal-list-ask = Will the measured specific heat of the metal match the table?
preset-specific-heat-metal-list-title = Finding the specific heat of a metal
preset-metal-controls-label = Metal
preset-mass-controls-label = Mass
preset-water-controls-label = Water
preset-build-question-2 = A cylinder of { $value } of { $mass } g was heated in boiling water to 100 °C and put into a calorimeter with { $water } g of water at 20 °C. We measure the temperature it all comes to and find the metal’s specific heat from the heat balance. Will it match the table?
preset-build-choices-4 = Yes, ≈ { $c } J/(kg·°C)
preset-build-choices-5 = Less than in the table
preset-build-choices-6 = More than in the table
